      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
          "Label": "BACKGROUND",
          "content": "Transvaginal evisceration following total vaginal hysterectomy secondary to coitus is extremely rare."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "CASE",
          "content": "A woman presented 10 months following a total vaginal hysterectomy with complaints of progressive postcoital abdominal and shoulder pain as well as a pinkish vaginal discharge. Examination revealed a 3-cm defect at the left edge of the vaginal cuff. Corrective surgery followed overnight observation with pain management."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Postcoital vaginal cuff disruption is rare, and complications can range from bowel evisceration to hemorrhage. Management should be tailored to the severity of the complications."
        }
      ]},
